   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThe jurisdiction this brief covers is the United States, and its sources are the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) and the Bureau of Transportation Statistics (BTS). The cost anchor is the BLS price release of Wednesday, May 13, 2026: «The Producer Price Index for final demand increased 1.4 percent in April, seasonally adjusted, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported today.» That follows «Final demand prices advanced 0.7 percent in March and 0.6 percent in February.» and BLS calls it «the largest advance since rising 1.7 percent in March 2022». The transport line is steeper than the whole: «The indexes for final demand transportation and warehousing services and for final demand services less trade, transportation, and warehousing also rose, 5.0 percent and 0.1 percent, respectively.» Against that, «The index for final demand services rose 1.2 percent in April, the largest increase since moving up 1.3 percent in March 2022.»\n\nThese are index changes in producer prices, not dollar rates: the release gives no rate for a shipment, a trucking mile or a pallet space, so a rate in currency for any lane is a gap here.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the 5.0 percent and 1.2 percent sentences, prices for transport and warehousing services rose faster in April 2026 than prices for services as a whole, so a rate review that starts from last quarter's transport prices starts from a lower base than the producer price index now shows. The sources name no competitor and no technology deployment, so this profile serves the cost, regulatory and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nThe captures state no verdict (possible, restricted or prohibited) on a specific operational choice in the United States, so the feasibility analysis here is inference from labour data. In its release of Friday, May 8, 2026, BTS reports the size of the road freight workforce: «Truck transportation rose to 1,469,600 in April 2026», and it was «up 0.3% from the previous month but down 1.4% from April 2025». The high is older: «truck October 2022 (1,588,600); warehousing and storage March 2022 (1,939,300)». For warehousing, «Warehousing and storage remained virtually unchanged in April 2026 at 1,830,700 from the previous month but down 2.7% from April 2025.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, the number of people employed in truck transportation and in warehousing and storage was lower in April 2026 than a year earlier and below the October 2022 and March 2022 highs, so adding capacity by hiring starts from a smaller workforce than at the peak. BTS does not say that the smaller workforce raised prices, so the link between the labour numbers and the price indexes is a gap.\n\n## 3. Cost Comparison Against Alternatives\n\nPrices that businesses pay each other for transport also rose. BLS says: «Half of the broad- based rise in April is attributable to prices for transportation and warehousing services for intermediate demand, which jumped 3.7 percent.» The whole index moved less: «The index for services for intermediate demand increased 1.1 percent in April, the largest advance since moving up 1.3 percent in March 2022.» For road freight the detail is sharper: «Over one-fifth of the April rise in the index for services for intermediate demand can be traced to an 8.1-percent increase in prices for truck transportation of freight.» The release adds that «courier, messenger, and U.S. postal services; freight forwarding; and fuels and lubricants retailing also moved higher», with no figure for freight forwarding. Fuel moved the same month: «a 7.8-percent jump in prices for final demand energy», and «Prices for jet fuel, diesel fuel, fresh and dry vegetables, industrial chemicals, and residual fuels also rose.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the 8.1-percent sentence, a forwarder that buys truck capacity is on the receiving side of an index that showed an 8.1-percent rise in April 2026 against 1.1 percent for all intermediate services, but the index is a national producer price and not a quote, so how far it reaches any one contract depends on that contract's terms, which the release does not state. The release does not say that the fuel increases caused the truck freight increase, so the fuel and freight moves are co-movement and not a stated cause, and the size of the freight forwarding move is a gap.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nThe April figures were preliminary in the BLS statements captured on June 5, 2026. BLS flagged its latest reading as preliminary: «Producer Price Index - Final Demand: +1.4%(p) in Apr 2026», and the release warns that «Some of the figures shown above and elsewhere in this release may differ from those previously reported because data for December through March have been revised to reflect the availability of late reports and corrections by respondents.» The twelve-month view is also on the page: «On an unadjusted basis, the index for final demand rose 6.0 percent for the 12 months ended in April, the largest 12-month increase since moving up 6.4 percent in December 2022.» On the labour side BLS projects «Total employment is projected to grow 3.1 percent between 2024 and 2034» for the economy as a whole, and the sector's own figure is in the table row «Total wage and salary employment 3.1 4,995.4 48-490 Transportation and warehousing 3.0 198.8», which the table sets out under percent change and numeric change in thousands headings. BTS adds the unemployment reading: «The unemployment rate in the U.S. transportation sector was 3.9% (not seasonally adjusted) in April 2026 according to the Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS).» It was up from last year: «In April 2026, the transportation sector unemployment rate rose 0.3 percentage points from 3.6% in April 2025.» and it sits below the national rate: «BLS reports that the U.S. unemployment rate, not seasonally adjusted, in April 2026 was 4.0% or 0.1 percentage points above the transportation sector rate.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the preliminary flag and the revision note, the April price moves were subject to revision as of the June 5, 2026 capture; the sources hold no later release, so whether the April figures were revised is a gap. Resting on the projection row, transportation and warehousing shows 3.0 in the projection table against 3.1 for all wage and salary employment, so the sources give no sign of the sector adding workers faster than the economy.\n\n- May 8, 2026: BTS reported the April 2026 transportation sector unemployment rate of 3.9%, up from 3.6% in April 2025.\n- May 13, 2026: BLS published the April 2026 Producer Price Index, with final demand transportation and warehousing services up 5.0 percent.\n- 2034: end of the BLS projection period, with transportation and warehousing shown at 3.0 in the 2024 to 2034 projection table against 3.1 for all wage and salary employment.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Producer Price Index news release, April 2026: https://www.bls.gov/news.release/ppi.nr0.htm\n- U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Major Economic Indicators: https://bls.gov/oes/current/naics2_48-49.htm\n- Bureau of Transportation Statistics, April 2026 U.S. Transportation Sector Unemployment: https://www.bts.gov/newsroom/april-2026-us-transportation-sector-unemployment-39-rises-above-april-2025-level-36\n- Bureau of Transportation Statistics, Latest Supply Chain and Freight Indicators: https://www.bts.gov/freight-indicators\n- U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Monthly Labor Review, Industry and occupational employment projections overview and highlights: https://www.bls.gov/opub/mlr/2026/article/industry-and-occupational-employment-projections-overview.htm\n",
