   "body": "## 1. Operational Cost Baseline for the Region\n\nThe jurisdiction this brief covers is Nunavut and the Canadian Arctic, and its sources are Crown-Indigenous Relations and Northern Affairs Canada, a 2026 review in Nature Reviews Earth and Environment and a 2013 post by the International Council on Clean Transportation (ICCT). The sources hold no current freight rate, port charge, fuel price or surcharge for Nunavut, so the cost baseline is mostly a gap. The one dated cost figure is old and is about routing, not Nunavut tariffs: in a post dated «October 2, 2013», the ICCT wrote that «The route through the Northwest Passage (NWP) in the U.S. and Canadian Arctic can shave 1,000 nautical miles off a trip that would otherwise go through the Panama Canal, equating to $80,000 in fuel cost savings.» A cost that Canadian rules do put on a vessel is insurance: «The MLA requires that vessels operating in waters under Canadian jurisdiction carry insurance for damages from oil spills.»\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the insurance duty applies to a given vessel and cargo is a question for counsel; the page states it for vessels in waters under Canadian jurisdiction generally, not only the Arctic, and names it on ship owners and operators.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the ICCT sentence, a 2013 fuel saving of $80,000 on one trip is a point from thirteen years before this brief and says nothing about today's fuel price, so it should not be used as a current figure; resting on the Marine Liability Act sentence, insurance for oil spill damage is a standing cost of operating in Canadian waters whose amount the sources do not give. The sources name no competitor and no technology deployment in Nunavut, so this profile serves the cost, regulatory and client lenses only.\n\n## 2. Feasibility of Specific Operational Choices\n\nOperating in Canadian Arctic waters is possible with extra conditions. The government page says: «Ships intending to operate within Canadian Arctic waters are subject to certain unique requirements in addition to those common to vessels operating elsewhere in Canada.» Reporting is tied to size: «The Northern Canada Vessel Traffic Services Zone Regulations require vessels operating in the Canadian Arctic with a gross tonnage (GT) of 300 tonnes or more, as well as vessels carrying a pollutant or dangerous good as cargo and vessels engaged in towing operations with a combined GT of 500 tonnes or more, to report identity, position and destination information to the Canadian Coast Guard.» Timing depends on ice: «The Zone Date System is based on historical ice conditions and a ship's ice class.» A ship that wants to sail outside it has a route: «Ship owners planning to operate outside of this prescriptive system have the option of using either the Arctic Ice Regime Shipping System (AIRSS) or the Polar Operational Limit Assessment Risk Indexing System (POLARIS).» For smaller ships there is a crew condition: «Canada requires that an ice navigator, a certified individual with experience operating in ice conditions, be present onboard ships under 500 GT that intend to operate outside of the Zone Date System.»\n\n*Legal Confirmation Required:* which of these duties applies to a given vessel or voyage (the gross tonnage thresholds, the cargo condition and the ice navigator condition) is a question for counsel or Transport Canada; the page states duties on ships and ship owners and none on a forwarder.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, a carrier of 300 tonnes or more gross tonnage bound for Nunavut waters reports to the Coast Guard, a ship outside the Zone Date System must choose AIRSS or POLARIS, and a ship under 500 GT in that case also carries an ice navigator, so route planning for cargo to or from the Arctic starts with the carrier's ship size and ice class, which the sources do not give for any carrier. The reporting centre is local: «It is administered by the Canadian Coast Guard's Marine Communications and Traffic Services Centre located at Iqaluit, Nunavut.»\n\n## 4. Cross-Regional Strategic Implications\n\nThe Arctic is a route alternative, and the 2013 ICCT post gave its logic against the Panama Canal: the Northwest Passage saves distance, and the post adds a condition of the route: «They also must transit at slower speeds because of the harsher conditions, which only increases efficiency.» Traffic is rising: the 2026 review finds that «the number of ships entering the Polar Code Arctic area increased by 37.3% between 2013 and 2023, increasing CO 2 emissions from shipping by 6.3%», and that «Sea ice retreat is opening up Arctic shipping routes, reducing transit times and enhancing access to regions with valuable resources.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those sentences, the shorter Northwest Passage transit competes with the Panama Canal routing on distance, as the ICCT post compares them, while the Canadian reporting and ice duties above and the emissions growth the review records are the counterweights, so a forwarder weighing an Arctic leg compares saved distance against those duties; the sources give no transit time or freight rate for a Nunavut leg, so the comparison cannot be put in numbers.\n\n## 7. Pending Changes That Shift the Calculus\n\nNo dated rule change is stated for Nunavut in the sources. The 2026 review sets a direction as its own view: «Sustainable Arctic shipping will require stricter regulations on fuel standards and greywater release alongside improvements in navigation and emission filtration technologies.» That is the review's recommendation and not an adopted rule. The older ICCT material records the position at its date: as of October 2, 2013, the ICCT wrote that «Despite the availability and proven effectiveness of technologies such as scrubbers, DPF, and low-sulfur fuel, they are not currently required aboard vessels transiting the Arctic or in near-Arctic waters.» The same post projected: «A recent report estimates increases in black carbon concentrations of 50% from shipping in the Arctic by 2030.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on the 2026 review sentence, stricter fuel and greywater rules for Arctic shipping are recommended and not yet stated as law in these sources, and the ICCT 2013 black carbon projection for 2030 is a projection from a 2013 report, so neither sets a date on which a Nunavut freight cost changes; the date of any new rule is a gap.\n\n- 2013: the ICCT post of October 2, 2013 projects a 50% rise in Arctic shipping black carbon concentrations by 2030.\n- 2030: the year of that 2013 projection.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- Crown-Indigenous Relations and Northern Affairs Canada, Shipping in the Arctic: https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1648145733596/1648145784817\n- Nature Reviews Earth and Environment (2026), review of Arctic shipping drivers and impacts: https://www.nature.com/articles/s43017-026-00790-2\n- International Council on Clean Transportation, Arctic sea shipping: Emissions matter more than you might think, October 2, 2013: https://theicct.org/arctic-sea-shipping-emissions-matter-more-than-you-might-think/\n",
